## File separate fixes

### Shared parser: month-first interpretation

For day-first tenants, the shared parser reads ambiguous dates month-first. R-41200 reports
an intended 7 April appearing as 4 July; R-41201 reports the reverse. The expected behavior
is to retain the intended date according to an explicit input contract.

See the [parser issue draft](../../../../issue-drafts/parser-issue.md). Verify the affected
input paths across tenant locales, with month-first inputs as controls. The export documents
reported symptoms and classification; it does not include a production parser to reproduce live.

### Booking-v3 form: hint and parser disagree

The form prints MM/DD/YYYY for day-first tenants while reading their input in tenant order.
A person following that hint can therefore get a swapped date even when tenant-aware parsing
works as designed. The affected tenant configurations are alder.physio, copper.studio and
pale.harbour. R-41230, R-41241 and R-41251 illustrate this path.

See the [form-hint issue draft](../../../../issue-drafts/hint-issue.md). Align the displayed hint
and accepted format, then verify booking-v3 separately from the shared parser. Include
booking-v2 and month-first locales as controls.

## Keep the timing anomalies visible

The desk's notes flag R-41202, R-41209, R-41236, R-41344 and R-41620. Their bodies describe
one-day differences, an invite apparently preceding a booking, or different days across locations.
They remain counted in the parser group under the supplied tenant-based classifier.

That filing rule does not prove their underlying cause. Preserve those report IDs for follow-up;
do not double-count them, subtract them silently, or call them a confirmed timezone defect.
Check original timestamps, timezone handling and reproduction evidence before deciding whether
another ticket is warranted.

## The queue did not begin with the merge

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports arrived before the large July merge and continued afterward. A spike in merges
describes desk activity, not necessarily a new product incident. The fact that an issue is
labelled known does not establish that it has been fixed or that reporting has stopped.

## Change the triage decision through a new record

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/) and the
[triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe/) were reviewed in June 2024. They tell the desk to merge
on matching subjects. The interactive examples below show why that rule loses information:
the same subject can hide different causes, and different subjects can describe one cause.

<DedupeExplorer />

Proposed replacement: compare tenant context, input path and evidence before merging.
Unknown tenants or unexplained symptoms need investigation rather than an automatic merge.
Keep the accepted historical decision intact. Record any replacement in a new decision record
after the desk accepts it, then update the triage guidance and review date together.

## Evidence and follow-up

- Counts and groups: reports-4471.json, tenant configuration files, causes.ts and locales.ts.
- Timing caveats: desk-notes.json and the original report bodies.
- Queue history: receivedAt and mergedAt, rendered by QueueTimeline.
- Recheck the live desk before implementation and again before declaring either issue resolved.

